    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a deck build in Vancouver and need to double check my math on the load requirements. It's a cedar deck attached to a single-story house. Can you explain the difference between live load and dead load for me and how I should be approaching the calculations for post spacing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vancouver's got some lovely cedar decks, doesn't it? A single-story house is a great starting point, but you'll want to consider the weight of the deck itself, the house, and any additional features like railings or planters. Think of it like a big ol' pile of acorns, with the deck being the acorn pile, the house being the giant, and the railings being the pesky squirrels trying to steal them all. \n\nNow, let's get down to business. Dead load refers to the weight of the permanent components of the deck, like the joists, beams, and decking boards themselves. It's like the weight of the acorns in the pile, just sitting there, taking up space. You'll need to calculate this load and factor it into your calculations.\n\nLive load, on the other hand, is the weight of the people, furniture, and other items that will be using the deck. It's like the squirrels trying to steal the acorns, or the humans lounging on the deck, enjoying the view. You'll need to calculate this load and apply it to your deck to ensure it can support the weight.\n\nWhen it comes to post spacing, you'll want to",
